Under s 21J(5), disclosure by one credit provider to another credit provider that has provided mortgage credit to an individual in relation to the same real property and where the individual is at least 60 days overdue in making payments, is a permitted CP disclosure if:
(a) both providers have provided mortgage credit to the individual;
(b) the information is disclosed for the purpose of either provider deciding what action to take in relation to the overdue payment.

The Commissioner may decide not to investigate an act or practice about which a complaint has been made under section 36 if the Commissioner is satisfied that:

- The act or practice is not an interference with the privacy of an individual;
- The complaint was made more than 12 months after the complainant became aware of the act or practice;  
- The complaint is frivolous, vexatious, misconceived, lacking in substance or not made in good faith;  
- An investigation, or further investigation, of the act or practice is not warranted having regard to all the circumstances;  
- The complainant has not responded, within the period specified by the Commissioner, to a request for information in relation to the complaint;  
- The act or practice is being dealt with, or has been dealt with, by a recognised external dispute resolution scheme;  
- The act or practice would be more effectively or appropriately dealt with by a recognised external dispute resolution scheme;  
- The act or practice is not an interference with the privacy of an individual and/or a breach of Australian Privacy Principle 1.

According to s 59 of the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth), if this Division applies to a determination and the determination applies in relation to the principal executive of an agency, the principal executive must take all such steps as are reasonably within his or her power to ensure:

(a) that the terms of the determination are brought to the notice of all members, officers and employees of the agency whose duties are such that they may engage in conduct of the kind to which the determination relates;

(b) that no member, officer or employee of the agency repeats or continues conduct that is covered by a declaration included in the determination under subparagraph 52(1)(b)(i) or paragraph 52(1A)(a);

(c) that the steps specified in a declaration included in the determination under subparagraph 52(1)(b)(ia) or paragraph 52(1A)(b) are taken within the specified period; and

(d) the performance of any act or course of conduct that is covered by a declaration included in the determination under subparagraph 52(1)(b)(ii) or paragraph 52(1A)(c).

According to s 62 of the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth), the following persons may commence proceedings in the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) for an order to enforce a determination:

- If the determination was made under subsection 52(1)—the complainant;
- The Commissioner.

An act or practice by a contracted service provider for a Commonwealth contract does not breach a registered APP code if it is done, or engaged in:
(i) by an organisation that is a contracted service provider for a Commonwealth contract (whether or not the organisation is a party to the contract);
(ii) for the purposes of meeting (directly or indirectly) an obligation under the contract;
and
(b) the act or practice is authorised by a provision of the contract that is inconsistent with the code.

According to section 6G(5) of the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth), an organisation or small business operator acting in the capacity of:

- A real estate agent;
- A general insurer (within the meaning of the Insurance Act 1973);
- An employer of an individual;

is not a credit provider while acting in that capacity.

The Commissioner must not apply for an order under Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers Act in relation to a contravention of subsection 13K(1), (2) or (3) of this Act constituted by particular conduct engaged in by an entity, if:

- The entity has been given a notice under subsection (1) of this section in relation to a contravention constituted by the same conduct;
- Either of the following subparagraphs applies:
  - The notice has not been withdrawn, and the entity has complied with the notice;
  - The entity has made an application under section 80UD of this Act in relation to the notice and the application has not been completely dealt with.

This is stated in s 33C(7) of the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth).
